lisa-design:
- Provider interface: GetAuthURL/ExchangeCode take a codeVerifier and a new UsesPKCE() reports whether the provider needs one. OIDC providers (BaseOIDCProvider, Google via embedding) return true; OAuth providers (BaseOAuthProvider, GitHub) return false and ignore the verifier, so OAuth behaviour is unchanged without name-based special cases.
- The handler generates the verifier (oauth2.GenerateVerifier) only when the service reports UsesPKCE(provider); it is stored in an `oauth_code_verifier` cookie with the same attributes as `oauth_state` (shared setSSOFlowCookie helper), and only its S256 hash reaches the IdP.
- Callback clears the verifier cookie unconditionally next to oauth_state (also cleans a verifier left by an abandoned OIDC login). For a PKCE provider a missing/empty verifier cookie redirects to /login?error=invalid_state (same code as a missing state cookie: same cause, 5-min expiry / other browser; no UI change needed) and never exchanges the code.
- BaseOIDCProvider.ExchangeCode refuses an empty verifier as defence in depth: no path can fall back to a non-PKCE exchange.
- public_client: oidc only (protocol oidc or empty), rejected at config validation for any other protocol; public_client with a non-empty client_secret is rejected too (fail closed instead of silently ignoring a configured secret). Public clients use oauth2.AuthStyleInParams (client_id in body, no secret, no Basic auth) instead of auto-detect.
- PublicClient field added at the end of SSOProviderConfig so gofmt does not realign every existing field.
- Mock OIDC server's /token implements the PKCE check (IssueCode binds a code to the challenge; mismatched or missing verifier -> invalid_grant) so tests fail on a wrong verifier rather than only asserting the form field.

